{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>For also.</b> He says that time and circumstance prevent a man from doing what he desires, and there is yet another hard thing: it is possible that death may come upon him suddenly and he does not know. He compared human beings to fish and birds, which do not know until they are caught and cannot escape." "<b>And the word יוקשים.</b> A descriptive noun. Rabbi Judah the Grammarian, of blessed memory, said: there are four words in Scripture and no fifth, on the pattern of פועלים yet they are פעולים, and they are: והסנה, איננו אוכל, אם אותי לוקח מאתך, ורגל מועדת — כהם יוקשים בני האדם. And Rabbi Jonah, of blessed memory, added a fifth word: לנער היולד. And Rabbi Samuel the Nagid said that the heh of היולד stands in place of אשר, like the heh of ההוללה, and a past-tense verb comes in place of future. Rabbi Moses the Kohen, of blessed memory, said that there are four only; therefore remove the word מועדת from the count and let the word היולד be in its place, because of the yod in their count: יוקשים, ותוכל, ולוקח, ויולד, from transitive verbs, while מועדת is from intransitive verbs. And he said that the shureq stands in place of cholam, and it ought to have been on the pattern of ובחומה היא יושבת, and the mem of פתאם is added unexpectedly, and from it פתאים, and likewise שלשום. And more correct is that the mem be a sign of the day, and תמול."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "The last clause 'and the mem be a sign of the day, and תמול' is textually opaque; translated hyper-literally. Ibn Ezra appears to be discussing the morphological function of the final mem in פתאם and similar forms, but the argument is compressed and possibly corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:46.484608033Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}